<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- miuix-blur declares minSdk 33; this app's floor is 26. The override is deliberate and
         safe only because every blur call site is version-gated inside `:design`'s
         `LiquidGlass` — below 33 nothing loads that package's classes. See NOTICE.md and
         docs/PHASE-3-PLAN.md §1. -->
    <uses-sdk tools:overrideLibrary="top.yukonga.miuix.kmp.blur" />

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <!-- An agent that is blocked on an approval or a question must be able to reach
         a pocketed phone; the in-app card alone cannot. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- The "Needs attention" channel asks to vibrate, so an approval or a question
         is felt rather than only shown. Install-time permission: no prompt, and
         nothing here vibrates on its own. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
    <!-- The standing connection: a foreground service is the only way to keep the
         event stream open with no Activity, which is what makes an agent's
         approval or question reach a pocketed phone. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <!--
      remoteMessaging, not dataSync.

      ## The crash this fixes

      Recorded on the device, at a cold start, on the main thread:

        RemoteServiceException$ForegroundServiceDidNotStopInTimeException:
          A foreground service of type dataSync did not stop within its timeout

      That is Android 15's foreground-service *time limit* landing on this app, not a
      rendering fault. AOSP states it plainly (ActiveServices.java):

        @EnabledSince(targetSdkVersion = VERSION_CODES.VANILLA_ICE_CREAM)
        static final long FGS_INTRODUCE_TIME_LIMITS = 317799821L;
        // "Mapping of uid to {fgs_type, fgs_info} for time limited fgs types such as
        //  dataSync and mediaProcessing."

      dataSync is one of the limited types: after its accumulated budget the system calls
      Service#onTimeout and then, if the service is still up a few seconds later, *kills
      the app*. With targetSdk 37 the gate is on, so this build was on a countdown from
      the first launch — which is why it died "at start-up" and why nothing about the UI
      changed the outcome.

      The app is not syncing data. It is holding an event stream open so an agent's
      approval or question reaches the phone — which is exactly what remoteMessaging is
      for, and remoteMessaging is **not** a time-limited type. The permission below is
      what that type requires.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_REMOTE_MESSAGING" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />

    <application
        android:name=".DshApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.DSH">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden|uiMode|density|fontScale"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.DSH"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Share to DSH. One image, one document, or several at once land in the
                 open session's composer as attachments, which is the same path the +
                 button takes — so a screenshot from the gallery or a PDF from a viewer
                 needs no detour through Downloads.

                 `*/*` on purpose: the app does not care what the file is, and a filter
                 listing only image/* would silently hide "DSH" from a share of
                 anything else. Both actions are registered because a single-file share
                 and a multi-file share are different intents, not a flag. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <service
            android:name=".DshConnectionService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="remoteMessaging" />

        <!--
          The homescreen widget. `exported="true"` because the platform requires the
          attribute to be explicit on any component with an intent-filter, and because
          it is what every shipped widget provider on the test handset declares; the
          only thing an outside caller can do with it is ask the card to draw itself
          again from a file this app wrote.

          No updatePeriodMillis timer and no WorkManager: see
          res/xml/dsh_widget_info.xml and uk.xa0.dsh.widget.WidgetPublisher for why a
          widget fed by a live socket has nothing to learn from a wake-up.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.DshWidgetProvider"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/dsh_widget_info" />
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>
